{"causes": ["The destructive and protracted Social War (220–217 BCE) between major Greek powers created a desperate need for peace and stability.", "The growing military and political influence of the Roman Republic in the western Mediterranean, particularly after the First Punic War, presented a clear external threat to Greek independence.", "The internal divisions and constant rivalries among the Hellenistic Greek states weakened their collective ability to resist foreign intervention.", "Philip V of Macedon's expansionist ambitions and his role in the Social War prompted Agelaus to advocate for a more unified, defensive stance for all Greeks."], "effects": ["Agelaus's diplomatic efforts culminated in the Peace of Naupactus in 217 BCE, temporarily ending the destructive Social War.", "His impassioned speech at Naupactus highlighted the emerging threat of Rome and Hannibal to the Greek world, though its warnings were largely unheeded in the long term.", "The concept of pan-Hellenic unity, though fleetingly achieved, was articulated as a vital defense against external powers, influencing later political thought."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "agelaus-of-naupactus", "sourceName": "Agelaus of Naupactus", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "peace-of-naupactus", "targetName": "Peace of Naupactus", "context": "Agelaus's diplomatic speech and efforts directly led to the negotiation and signing of the Peace of Naupactus in 217 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "polybius", "sourceName": "Polybius", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "agelaus-of-naupactus", "targetName": "Agelaus of Naupactus", "context": "Polybius's 'Histories' preserved Agelaus's famous speech at Naupactus, ensuring his place in the historical record for later generations."}, {"sourceSlug": "agelaus-of-naupactus", "sourceName": "Agelaus of Naupactus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "first-macedonian-war", "targetName": "First Macedonian War", "context": "Agelaus served as a leading official of the Aetolian League during the First Macedonian War (215–205 BCE), navigating its complex alliances."}, {"sourceSlug": "agelaus-of-naupactus", "sourceName": "Agelaus of Naupactus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "naupactus-greece", "targetName": "Naupactus, Greece", "context": "Agelaus delivered his pivotal speech and presided over the peace congress in his home city of Naupactus in 217 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "agelaus-of-naupactus", "sourceName": "Agelaus of Naupactus", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "philip-v-of-macedon", "targetName": "Philip V of Macedon", "context": "Agelaus's address at Naupactus directly challenged Philip V to abandon his expansionist policies and instead lead a united Greek defense."}], "places": [{"name": "Naupactus, Greece", "role": "Hometown; Congress Site"}, {"name": "Aetolia, Greece", "role": "Political Base; Region"}, {"name": "Pella, Macedon", "role": "Rival Power Center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Agelaus of Naupactus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "agelaus-of-naupactus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1073743", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agelaus_of_Naupactus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Agelaus's prescient warning about Roman power and his advocacy for pan-Hellenic unity at Naupactus represented a rare moment of foresight in a fragmented Greek world. Although his vision was not fully realized, his speech, preserved by Polybius, serves as a crucial historical document illustrating the geopolitical challenges facing the Hellenistic states before their eventual subjugation.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-18T21:16:31Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39166724510", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Agelaus of Naupactus was a leading official in the Aetolian League, renowned for his efforts to promote peace in Naupactus. He was notably an advocate of the concept of brotherhood among all Greeks.", "newValue": "Agelaus of Naupactus, a prominent statesman and strategos of the Aetolian League in the late 3rd century BCE, rose to historical prominence during the First Macedonian War (215–205 BCE). Though his exact birth and death dates remain unrecorded, his political career is firmly situated between 221 BCE and 210 BCE, primarily within the Aetolian city of Naupactus. Agelaus distinguished himself not through military conquest, but through an impassioned and prescient plea for Greek unity and peace, delivered at the Congress of Naupactus in 217 BCE, a moment that underscored the growing external threats to Hellenic autonomy.

His most celebrated contribution was his address at the Congress of Naupactus, convened to negotiate an end to the Social War (220–217 BCE) between the Aetolian League, Sparta, and Elis against the Achaean League, Macedon, and their allies. Agelaus, then strategos of the Aetolian League, urged the warring Greek states to cease their internecine conflicts, warning them of the rising power of Rome in the west and the growing threat of Hannibal Barca in the east. He famously articulated the concept of a united Greek front, a 'common brotherhood,' against these emergent superpowers, advocating for King Philip V of Macedon to act as a unifying leader rather than a regional hegemon. His diplomatic efforts led to the Peace of Naupactus, temporarily ending the Social War.

While the immediate impact of his vision for pan-Hellenic unity was limited, as Greek states soon resumed their rivalries and entangled themselves in Roman affairs, Agelaus's speech stands as a profound moment of foresight. He articulated a geopolitical reality that would soon engulf the Hellenistic world, with Rome ultimately subjugating all Greek states. His words resonate as a poignant, if ultimately unheeded, warning against internal division in the face of overwhelming external power, making him a crucial, if tragic, figure in the narrative of Greek independence."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-18T21:16:31Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39166724510", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The destructive and protracted Social War (220–217 BCE) between major Greek powers created a desperate need for peace and stability.", "The growing military and political influence of the Roman Republic in the western Mediterranean, particularly after the First Punic War, presented a clear external threat to Greek independence.", "The internal divisions and constant rivalries among the Hellenistic Greek states weakened their collective ability to resist foreign intervention.", "Philip V of Macedon's expansionist ambitions and his role in the Social War prompted Agelaus to advocate for a more unified, defensive stance for all Greeks."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-18T21:16:31Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39166724510", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks that shaped subsequent civili", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical